[
    {
        "Name": "failure_forensics",
        "Title": "Failure Forensics: Predicting Dominant Deployment Failure Modes from Pre-Deployment Signatures",
        "Short Hypothesis": "The dominant failure mode a deep learning model will exhibit under deployment is predictable from a small set of measurable signatures of the model, its training data, and its training dynamics. This is the best setting because it directly addresses the ICBINB question of why applied DL underperforms: instead of merely documenting failures after deployment, we propose a pre-deployment diagnostic that tells practitioners which failure mode (e.g., sensitivity collapse, false-positive inflation, calibration drift, subgroup disparity, spurious reliance) is likely to dominate. Simpler alternatives\u2014such as standard robustness benchmarks\u2014only report aggregate performance drops and cannot answer which failure mode will occur, while domain-specific post-hoc analyses (e.g., medical imaging outlier studies) are too narrow to yield generalizable predictions.",
        "Related Work": "Existing work has documented failure modes in isolated settings: Jajal et al. (2024) analyze ONNX converter failures; Fan et al. (2025) characterize outliers in commercial autosegmentation; Shuaibu et al. (2026) show geographic domain shift can produce divergent failure modes (false-positive surges vs. sensitivity collapse); Hubbertz et al. (2026) disentangle memorization vs. geometry overfitting in online mapping. Robustness benchmarks (e.g., RobustBench, WILDS, ImageNet-C) measure performance under shifts but do not predict failure modes. Our proposal differs by (1) treating failure modes as a multi-label prediction problem, (2) deriving signatures from model/data/training properties before deployment, and (3) cross-validating predictions on real-world post-deployment failure reports. This is a new predictive framing, not a trivial extension of existing taxonomies.",
        "Abstract": "Deep learning models frequently fail in deployment in ways that benchmark accuracy does not reveal: a screening model may lose sensitivity under domain shift, a segmentation tool may produce fragmented contours on contrast-enhanced scans, or a classifier may become overconfident on out-of-distribution inputs. Current practice documents these failures after the fact, often in domain-specific studies, but offers no general way to anticipate which failure mode will dominate before deployment. We propose Failure Forensics, a framework that predicts the dominant failure mode of a model from measurable pre-deployment signatures. We build a cross-domain atlas of failure modes by systematically applying a standardized battery of deployment stressors\u2014covariate shift, domain shift, label noise, class imbalance, spurious correlations, adversarial perturbations, and data scarcity\u2014to a diverse set of tasks (image classification, medical imaging, NLP, tabular, time series, segmentation). For each model, we compute signatures from model internals (representation geometry, gradient norms, prediction entropy, calibration), training data (label noise estimates, spurious correlation strength, class balance, diversity), and training dynamics (loss landscape sharpness, memorization scores). A meta-classifier learns to map these signatures to failure-mode labels. We validate the framework with leave-one-task-out cross-validation and on real-world deployment reports, including a large-scale chest X-ray screening study and commercial autosegmentation outlier analyses. If successful, Failure Forensics would enable practitioners to run a pre-deployment 'failure risk check' and choose mitigation strategies before models reach the field. Even if prediction proves difficult, the resulting failure-mode atlas provides a valuable negative result for the community.",
        "Experiments": "1) Build a failure-mode atlas benchmark: use 8-10 public tasks spanning domains\u2014CIFAR-10/ImageNet subset (vision), ChestX-ray14/RetinaMNIST (medical), SST-2/SNLI (NLP), Adult/Credit (tabular), UCR time series, Cityscapes (segmentation), Pascal VOC (detection). For each task, train or fine-tune standard models (ResNet, ViT, BERT, TabNet, etc.) under controlled deficiencies: label noise (random/structured), class imbalance, spurious correlation, limited data, and standard training. 2) Apply a standardized battery of deployment stressors: covariate shift (blur, noise, contrast, weather), domain shift (e.g., CIFAR-10 to CIFAR-10.1, MNIST to SVHN, synthetic to real), concept drift, adversarial perturbations (FGSM/PGD), and data scarcity. Record failure-mode labels for each model-stressor combination: accuracy collapse, calibration failure (ECE), subgroup disparity, robustness degradation, spurious feature reliance, overconfidence, underconfidence, representation collapse. 3) Compute pre-deployment signatures: (a) model signatures\u2014CKA between layers, effective dimensionality, spectral decay of feature covariance, gradient norms, prediction entropy on validation, calibration curve, margin distribution; (b) data signatures\u2014class balance, confident-learning label noise estimates, mutual information between labels and protected attributes, distributional diversity/coverage; (c) training dynamics\u2014Hessian trace/sharpness approximation, memorization score via influence functions or training loss on outliers. 4) Train a meta-classifier (gradient boosting or small MLP) on signature vectors to predict failure-mode labels. Use leave-one-task-out cross-validation to test generalization. 5) Validate on real-world deployment case studies: use publicly available post-deployment failure reports (e.g., the UAE chest X-ray visa screening study with 1.3M images, commercial autosegmentation outlier analyses) to test whether signatures predict observed failure modes. 6) Baselines: random predictor, majority class, aggregate robustness metrics (ECE, OOD AUROC), and a performance-only predictor. Ablate signature groups (model vs. data vs. training dynamics). Metrics: failure-mode classification accuracy, macro-F1, per-mode AUROC, calibration of meta-predictions, and early-warning utility (whether predicted dominant failure mode matches actual worst degradation). Use SHAP to extract interpretable rules from the meta-model.",
        "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Failure modes are not mutually exclusive; multi-label prediction may be noisy, and the meta-classifier could learn benchmark-specific artifacts rather than generalizable signatures. Leave-one-task-out validation may be limited by task heterogeneity, and some failure modes (e.g., ethical/societal harms) are hard to quantify with controlled stressors. Computing loss-landscape curvature and influence functions can be expensive; we will use approximations (e.g., Hutchinson trace estimator, random projections) to keep costs feasible. Real-world validation depends on the availability of detailed post-deployment reports, which are often sparse. The framework may reveal that signatures are not predictive\u2014but this negative result would itself be a valuable ICBINB contribution, motivating deeper study of what information is actually available before deployment."
    }
]